{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command \"python -X utf8 -c \\\"import subprocess,tomllib,json; c='3fe6290425f165abbd418862ddc2bd0fa7bc0491'; read=lambda p:tomllib.loads(subprocess.check_output(['git','show',c+':'+p]).decode()); n=read('docs/requirements/stakeholder-needs.toml'); [print(k,json.dumps(n['need'][k],ensure_ascii=False)) for k in ['SN-004','SN-008','SN-029']]; d=read('docs/requirements/interfaces.toml'); print('ROOT',list(d)); [print(k,json.dumps(d['interface'][k],ensure_ascii=False)) for k in ['IF-073','IF-074','IF-091','IF-224','IF-287']]\\\"; rg -n -A 30 -B 5 'verdict block|REVIEWER|CRITIC|Audit log|VERDICT:' project-trajectory/PROCESS.md project-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md docs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md\"","aggregated_output":"SN-004 {\"status\": \"Approved\", \"stakeholder_refs\": [\"STK-02\"], \"need\": \"A team advances only through **explicit approval gates** (DevStg-Reqs→DevStg-Tests→DevStg-Impl→…), and a gate passes only when its mechanical bar is met.\", \"why\": \"*Explicit approval gates so you can trust what ships.*\", \"priority\": \"M\", \"acceptance\": \"A harness run for a named gate enforces that gate's required steps; a missing required tool **fails**, never silently skips.\"}\r\nSN-008 {\"status\": \"Approved\", \"stakeholder_refs\": [\"STK-03\"], \"need\": \"A reader can believe a **pass verdict**: gates are **honest**, and a pass verdict never hides a skipped check, a stub, or an unmet declared criterion.\", \"why\": \"*Trust what ships*; a false pass is worse than a failure. The verdict is named as a verdict — the word PASS — never by hue: a colour word as the only name for the system's most important signal does not exist for a substantial class of readers, and the kit's console output carries zero ANSI colour in any case.\", \"priority\": \"M\", \"acceptance\": \"The harness **fails** (not skips) on a missing required tool — the explicitly-requested `--lenient` local mode is the one sanctioned degrade to SKIP (SR-006), never a CI/gate default; the no-stub detector and the privacy/secrets floor run at their declared gate.\"}\r\nSN-029 {\"status\": \"Approved\", \"stakeholder_refs\": [\"STK-01\"], \"tags\": [\"unattended\", \"loop\"], \"need\": \"**An autonomous run gets as far as it honestly can.** Once triggered, the coordinator stops for a human judgement only when the declared approval level reserves that tier for a human, when a round cannot converge on its own, or when requirement/test documentation is introduced or amended such that the gate drops below what automation is permitted to attest. On every tier released to automation, an approval still happens as a review from a declared perspective against the row's actual evidence, and leaves a record naming who or what approved it — releasing a tier means the review is delegated, never skipped.\", \"why\": \"The unattended layer only pays for itself if a walk-away run actually walks away — so what stops it must be exactly the set of judgements the owner reserved, and nothing else. A three-value gate-authority enum (`attended` / `single-approve` / `autonomous`) cannot express \\\"TCs are human-held but LLRs are not\\\", the distinction an owner actually wants to dial, and leaves each consuming table free to re-interpret the same word with its own fail-safe direction. Answering \\\"has this been approved?\\\" by walking git for the newest commit where a row reads `Verified` is sound only while every amendment flips its row in the same commit — precisely the sanctioned path an amendment detector ignores — so the blessed way to amend an attested requirement is invisible to every consumer, and a stakeholder need has no anchor at all (an SN has no Status cell).\", \"priority\": \"S\", \"acceptance\": \"A cumulative level names the highest tier a human still approves, compared against a separately derived SPINE STAGE (which tier is in process) via a declared, auditable mapping. Every failure direction — an unreadable stage, an out-of-range level, a wrong-typed dial — resolves toward MORE human involvement, because the failure that matters is a machine approving something a human meant to hold. Each acceptance is anchored ON THE ACCEPTED ARTIFACT'S OWN ROW — the commit whose tree carries the accepted text, plus a digest of that row's normative cells — never in a second registry keyed on the same artifact. Text that has moved away from what was accepted surfaces regardless of any Status movement, the one signal the sanctioned amend-and-flip path would otherwise hide, so an AMENDED requirement drops the derived stage exactly as a newly introduced one does. Each automated approval on a released tier appends a durable record (row, transition, acting reviewer, commit) distinguishable from a human approval by query.\"}\r\nROOT ['interface']\r\nIF-073 {\"owner\": \"docs/requirements/open-items.toml\", \"consumers\": [\"scripts/gen_open_items\", \"external:downstream adopter\"], \"channel\": \"file\", \"data\": \"status: pending | ruled; queued work-item needs edges cite pending items\", \"version\": \"v1\", \"status\": \"Drafted\", \"interface_to_external\": \"B-05\", \"coincident\": \"The format is what the adopter writes and every reader parses; a copy that reads as this format declares is the whole of what the seam delivers, and what the rows say is judged at their own tiers.\", \"notes\": \"A pending item has at least one queued work-item citer. A ruling updates every citing row in the same commit: its Done-when adds a criterion citing the item, its SpecRef must name a real specification once every item it cites is non-pending, and any other changed field is synchronized. The queued citer is the item's placeholder; wi_refs is historical metadata. Published contract, not a single consumer: kit scripts parse the registry and downstream adopters author decisions.\"}\r\nIF-074 {\"owner\": \"scripts/gen_open_items\", \"consumers\": [\"external:downstream adopter\"], \"channel\": \"file\", \"data\": \"docs/open-items.html — cited pending decisions, uncited items, approval/re-attest chains and owing needs and stakeholders, verdict audit, owner actions; Decisions to review last\", \"version\": \"v1\", \"status\": \"Drafted\", \"interface_to_external\": \"B-05\", \"coincident\": \"The page lists the pending decisions and the re-attestation chains for the owner to rule on; showing them is the page's whole promise, and the ruling is the authority crossing's own.\"}\r\nIF-091 {\"owner\": \"scripts/acceptance_record\", \"requestors\": [\"scripts/intake\", \"scripts/integrate\"], \"channel\": \"call\", \"data\": \"Approval delta; merge_approval_refusal(root, base, head, metas, trunk) -> acceptance or refusal; settled_amendments(...) -> (settled ids, refusal)\", \"version\": \"v1\", \"status\": \"Drafted\", \"component\": \"CMP-008\"}\r\nIF-224 {\"rationale\": \"The owner's approval brief sets apart the owing chains the human-approval dial releases, and which rows the dial releases is a question the loop already answers for the rows it mints and the adjudication brief it composes. Reading that one predicate keeps the owner's surface, the mint and the adjudicator agreeing about who signs a row; a rung table in the brief would be a third answer to one question, free to disagree with the other two.\", \"owner\": \"scripts/agent_common\", \"requestors\": [\"scripts/trace\"], \"channel\": \"call\", \"data\": \"human_approves_spine(docs, registry) -> True when the dial holds the registry's rung or no rung map names it; False when the dial releases it; trunk_name(root) -> declared trunk or TrunkUndeclared; parse_wi_id(value, source) -> one WI id or ValueError\", \"version\": \"v1\", \"status\": \"Drafted\", \"component\": \"CMP-008\", \"notes\": \"Cross-component crossing: trace is CMP-006 and agent_common is CMP-008. The imports are deferred: approval-brief rendering reads human_approves_spine, while a reservation reads trunk_name and parse_wi_id; trace paths outside those uses load nothing from the coordinator's primitives. The shipped dial holds every rung, so a repo that has released none sees the brief it saw before.\"}\r\nIF-287 {\"rationale\": \"Combined brief composition, review verdict checking, merge-time disposition and approval acts need one interpretation of their machine lines, kind-scoped tokens, bound row identities and sections; separate parsers could let one judgement act outside its requested scope or let a malformed review verdict reach a different consumer.\", \"owner\": \"scripts/kitlib/sitting\", \"requestors\": [\"scripts/adjudicate_brief\", \"scripts/amendment_brief\", \"scripts/acceptance_record\", \"scripts/consolidate\", \"scripts/intake\", \"scripts/review_brief\", \"scripts/score_reviews\", \"scripts/plan_coverage\", \"scripts/adjudicate_act\", \"scripts/held_status\"], \"channel\": \"call\", \"data\": \"binding/path grammar; requested, identity and findings-file cells; outcome; combined-scope and review-verdict readers\", \"version\": \"v1\", \"status\": \"Drafted\", \"component\": \"CMP-008\", \"notes\": \"source - consumes no declared seam; pure functions. An identity cell is preserved when a pending binding is rewritten as accepted or failed; absent, malformed or repeated identities read as no identity with a reason. A findings-file record is carried the same way when a pending binding is rewritten as accepted or failed; an absent record reads as none.\"}\r\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-22-`DELTA:` line: does the work item add a state, add a value to a domain, make\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-23-an output depend on a new input, or cross a new boundary, and if so does the\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-24-plan carry the change within its scope?\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-25-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-26-Every verdict **cites anchor ids**. The critic's verdict line is\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md:27:`VERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=N`; the arbiter's is\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md:28:`VERDICT: SELECT <plan> ports=N`, each port a cited delta\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-29-(\"port B-3 — closes C4, anchored DevStg-Tests\").\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-30-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-31-**Transfer caveat (state wherever this rubric is applied):** the\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-32-debate/selection evidence behind this protocol comes from QA, math, and code\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-33-with objective verifiers — nothing benchmarks it on *plan artifacts*, and\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-34-two-planner reconciliation evaluated for plan quality is an open research gap.\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-35-This rubric is the best-supported extrapolation, not a proven instrument\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-36-([co-planning knowledge pack](../knowledge/co-planning.md), retrieved\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-37-2026-07-16).\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-38-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-39-## Anchors\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-40-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-41-**DevStg-Reqs — Solvable unit.** Each proposed WI is completable by one build session at\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-42-its tier: a first-time implementer could name the deliverable and a checkable\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-43-done-condition from the row alone. *Bad:* \"improve the dispatch layer\" (no\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-44-deliverable); a row whose scope needs three sessions and two design rulings.\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-45-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-46-**DevStg-Tests — Complete coverage.** Every goal clause is covered by some WI **or its\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-47-exclusion is declared** in the plan's notes as a non-goal with a reason —\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-48-never silence. The coverage report computes the gap; the critic judges whether\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-49-a declared exclusion is honest.\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-50-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-51-**DevStg-Impl — Non-redundant decomposition.** No two WIs cover the same clause without\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-52-a declared split reason; scope boundaries are crisp enough that two\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-53-implementers would not collide. Multi-covered clauses in the coverage report\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-54-are the tripwire; the judgment is whether the overlap is a real seam or a\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-55-duplicated scope.\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-56-\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-57-**G4 — Coherent DAG.** Every predecessor edge reflects a real artifact-level\ndocs/rubrics/plan-decomposition.md-58-dependency (an edge the planner could defend by naming what the successor\n--\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-941-Reviews use this block — in `log.md`, or as a `docs/reviews/WI-<n>-<PHASE>.md`\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-942-verdict file under the review layer (work-item-scoped names; serial counters\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-943-race under concurrency):\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-944-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-945-```\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md:946:### <HAT or REVIEWER> — <Gate> — Round <r> — <YYYY-MM-DD>\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md:947:VERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=<N>\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-948-Findings:\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-949-- [BLOCKER|MAJOR|MINOR] <ID or area> → <issue> → <suggested change> → @<owner>\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-950-```\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-951-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-952-Sitting sign-offs live in the **Sittings** table in `log.md`; the driver\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-953-records the gate decision there, cites it from status.md's *Stage* line,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-954-and requests acceptance per the declared gate authority (§4).\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-955-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-956-**Voice policy — warmth has a layer boundary.** Personality is a human value, not\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-957-a machine one: **human-facing** output (CLI narration, a kickoff greeting, a\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-958-release-checklist intro) may carry warmth and **dry wit at most**;\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-959-**machine/agent-facing** output — this protocol's findings and verdicts,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-960-subagent prompts, registry cells, commit messages — stays **literal, terse,\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-961-structured: no whimsy**. Levity there costs tokens, reads ambiguously to the\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-962-next agent (irony/understatement is exactly what a parser misreads), and\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-963-erodes the honesty/severity signal this protocol depends on. Default voice is\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-964-**restrained**\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-965-(\"direct and concrete; dry wit at most; never at the expense of clarity or\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-966-honesty\"); a project may expose an optional, named **tone knob** to dial levity\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-967-up or down — never a baked-in persona, since no single tone fits both a\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-968-medical-device repo and a game studio.\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-969-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-970-### Change intake — routing a problem to the spine\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-971-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-972-An inbound problem routes by **which registry row it contradicts** — that\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-973-classification, not the fix, is step 1. Then check the parent obligation and\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-974-selected design before treating the violation as a coverage gap:\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-975-\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-976-```mermaid\nproject-trajectory/PROCESS.md-977-flowchart TD\n--\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-65-## Output\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-66-\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-67-One block, nothing else:\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-68-\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-69-```\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md:70:VERDICT: APPROVE|CHANGES-REQUESTED findings=N\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-71-- [<anchor>] <plan row>: <the defect, one or two sentences, and what would\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-72-  satisfy the anchor>\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-73-...\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-74-```\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-75-\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-76-`CHANGES-REQUESTED` if any finding is blocking; findings=0 with APPROVE is a\nproject-trajectory/prompts/dual-plan-critic.template.md-77-legitimate outcome — do not invent findings to look thorough.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
